  "interpretation": "The patch refactors app/db.py to replace the linear string-scan tag filtering (INSTR) with a normalized many-to-many schema (note_tags table) and index-based joins. Validation passed, confirming functional correctness and API contract preservation. However, the runner rejected the change due to insufficient improvement. The candidate median latency (0.481ms) improved by only 2.13% over the parent (0.561ms), falling short of the acceptance threshold. While the candidate reduced noise (CV 0.46% vs 1.12%) and max latency, the mean latency remained nearly identical (0.531ms vs 0.543ms). The optimization primarily benefits list_notes, which dominates the workload (50% of operations), but the overhead of maintaining the note_tags table in create/update/delete operations likely offsets the read gains for this specific dataset size and access pattern. The hypothesis that index joins would significantly outperform string scans was not validated by the measured relative improvement.",
  "limitations": [
    "The 2.13% improvement is below the implicit acceptance threshold for 'sufficient improvement'.",
    "Measurements reflect a specific seed/workload; results may not generalize to larger datasets or different access patterns.",
    "The patch introduces write overhead for tag synchronization, which may negate read benefits in write-heavy scenarios.",
    "No statistical significance testing is provided to confirm the improvement is not due to noise."
  ],
